<script>
// GET /backstage/benchmarks — the whole of RFC-0104's dataset (M3).
//
// `/compare` carries the chart and the eight-row table. This page carries what a
// chart cannot: both measurement runs cell by cell, the ten raw readings behind
// every median, the drift between the runs, the environment down to the
// wasmtime sha256, the named cause under every deviation, the work items each
// cause produced, and the game's own published ceiling with the date it was read.
//
// It is a BACKSTAGE page for the reason the record pages are: this is how the
// number was decided, not whether to use the language. `layout="none"`, the
// backstage masthead, no consumer navigation, no soft navigation.
//
// Everything numeric here is read from `rfcs/bench-0104/results/*.json` while
// the site builds — see `app/bench.vyrn`. The prose that is a conclusion rather
// than a measurement (the causes, the work items) is in that module too, marked
// as such, and the RFC it was drawn from is one link away.
layout="none"
import { Head } from "std/ui"
import { backstageHead, backstageNav, backstageFooter, rfcHref, rfcTag } from "../../backstage"
import { tableRows, detailRows, envRows, floorRows, causeRows, workItems, ceilingRows, ceilingFetched, benchMachine, benchDate, benchRuns, benchMethod, worstDrift, steadyCells, Reading, Row, Detail } from "../../bench"
import { shellHtml } from "../../code"

export fn head() -> Head {
    return backstageHead("The benchmark dataset — Vyrn backstage")
}

/// The record this page is the data behind.
fn recordHref() -> String {
    return rfcHref(104)
}

fn recordTag() -> String {
    return rfcTag(104)
}

/// The two files the whole page is read from.
fn recordFiles() -> Array<String> {
    return [
        "rfcs/bench-0104/results/2026-08-19-LOCUST-v2.json",
        "rfcs/bench-0104/results/2026-08-19-LOCUST-v2-run2.json",
    ]
}

/// The command that produced them, for a reader who wants to disagree.
fn harnessCommand() -> String {
    return "python rfcs/bench-0104/harness/run.py"
}
</script>

<template>
<div id="root" class="backstage">
    <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <header class="masthead backstage" v-html='backstageNav("record")'></header>
    <div id="main" class="skip-target" tabindex="-1"></div>
    <main class="page">
        <aside class="rail">
            <a href="#environment"><span class="n">01</span>the machine</a>
            <a href="#runs"><span class="n">02</span>both runs</a>
            <a href="#causes"><span class="n">03</span>the causes</a>
            <a href="#work"><span class="n">04</span>the work</a>
            <a href="#ceiling"><span class="n">05</span>the ceiling</a>
        </aside>

        <section class="hero">
            <p class="eyebrow">Backstage — {{ recordTag() }}</p>
            <h1 class="display">The benchmark dataset, in full</h1>
            <p class="lede">Eight Benchmarks Game programs, five contestants, two complete measurement runs, ten timed runs per cell. The chart on the comparison page plots the first run's medians; everything under them is here. Read from the committed records while this page was built — <a :href="recordHref()">{{ recordTag() }}</a> is the argument that produced them.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="environment">
            <p class="eyebrow">01 — the machine</p>
            <h2>What "measured" means here</h2>
            <p>One machine, one afternoon, twice. A benchmark that does not publish this is an advertisement, so it is the first thing on the page rather than a footnote at the bottom of it.</p>
            <p class="notice"><strong>The machine was not quiet.</strong> A training job held one core of twelve and 7.2 GB of memory throughout both runs. It shows in the process floor below — an empty C program costs 7.8 ms here against 4.1 ms on the earlier dataset — and it moved four of the forty cells more than 10% off that dataset: k-nucleotide on C, Rust and node, and fasta on node. All four are the smallest readings in the set, where a doubled start-up is a large share of the number. Nothing was dropped and no method changed; the ratios this page publishes are computed from what the machine actually did.</p>
            <div class="scroller">
            <table class="matrix">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>What</th><th>As the record states it</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr v-for="row in envRows()" :key="row[0]">
                        <th scope="row">{{ row[0] }}</th>
                        <td><code>{{ row[1] }}</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            </div>
            <p class="cap">Measured {{ benchDate() }}. Method: {{ benchMethod() }}, {{ benchRuns() }} runs per cell.</p>

            <h3>The process floor</h3>
            <p>Every reading is whole-process wall time, which is the game's own convention and includes start-up — and for the WebAssembly leg it includes wasmtime compiling the module. An empty program in each contestant is what a reader subtracts.</p>
            <div class="scroller">
            <table class="matrix">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th v-for="f in floorRows()" :key="f.key"><span :class="f.tone">{{ f.name }}</span></th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td v-for="f in floorRows()" :key="f.key">{{ f.ms }}</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            </div>
            <p class="cap">An empty program, ten runs, median. It is what a reader subtracts, and it is why the smallest cells in the set carry a noise flag wherever they are printed: k-nucleotide's C and Rust legs are one to two floors of work, so nothing about them can be read to better than a few milliseconds.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="runs">
            <p class="eyebrow">02 — both runs</p>
            <h2>Every cell, twice, with the readings under it</h2>
            <p>The milestone's gate was that the committed dataset reproduces on a second run within stated noise. This is where that is checked rather than asserted: both medians, the drift between them, the within-run spread, and the ten raw readings the first median came from.</p>
            <p class="notice">Across all forty cells the second run moved by a median of 0.9% and a worst case of <strong>{{ worstDrift() }}</strong>. <strong>{{ steadyCells() }}</strong> cells moved by less than five per cent. The three that moved most are k-nucleotide's C, Rust and node legs — the three smallest readings in the set, all of them one to two process floors of work. So the noise band is ±10% on a median: every ratio the record calls a deviation is far outside it, and the one row that sits on the line says so instead of picking a side.</p>

            <div class="plate" v-for="r in tableRows()" :key="r.key" :id="r.key">
                <div class="plate-head">
                    <span class="pill live">Measured</span>
                    <span class="eyebrow"><a :href="r.game">{{ r.name }}</a> — N = {{ r.n }}</span>
                </div>
                <div class="stage">
                <div class="scroller">
                <table class="matrix bench">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Contestant</th><th>Run one</th><th>Run two</th><th>Drift</th><th>cpu secs</th><th>mem</th><th>gz</th><th>make secs</th><th>Spread</th><th>The ten readings of run one</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr v-for="d in detailRows(r.key)" :key="d.key">
                            <th scope="row">{{ d.name }}</th>
                            <td>{{ d.one }}</td>
                            <td>{{ d.two }}</td>
                            <td>{{ d.drift }}</td>
                            <td>{{ d.cpu }}</td>
                            <td>{{ d.mem }}</td>
                            <td>{{ d.gz }}</td>
                            <td>{{ d.make }}</td>
                            <td>{{ d.spread }}<span v-if="d.noisy" class="flag"> noisy</span></td>
                            <td class="runs"><code>{{ d.runs }}</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="cap">Drift is the second run's median against the first. A spread is the widest reading minus the narrowest, as a percentage of the median, within one run. The four columns after it are the game's own: cpu time is user plus kernel of the child process, mem is its peak working set, gz is the gzipped source, make is the build. Everything on this page is read from <code>{{ recordFiles()[0] }}</code> and <code>{{ recordFiles()[1] }}</code> while the site is built.</p>
            <p class="cap">Windows accounts process cpu time in 15.625-millisecond ticks, so a cpu figure is that granular and the two ten-millisecond cells resolve to one tick or none. The wall clock is not: it is <code>perf_counter</code>, and it is what every ratio on either page is computed from.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="causes">
            <p class="eyebrow">03 — the causes</p>
            <h2>Every deviation, named</h2>
            <p>The claim {{ recordTag() }} opened with is that every measured deviation is a named defect or a named missing feature, never a shrug. Five of the eight rows deviate from Rust beyond the noise band. Three do not, and they say so rather than being quietly left out.</p>
            <ul class="plain">
                <li v-for="c in causeRows()" :key="c[0]">
                    <div class="cmd"><code>{{ c[0] }} — {{ c[1] }}</code></div>
                    <span class="note">{{ c[2] }}</span>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="work">
            <p class="eyebrow">04 — the work</p>
            <h2>What the causes turned into</h2>
            <p>A benchmark that loses is a diagnosis with a program attached. These are the eleven items the measurements produced — each one a fixable cause named above, none of them in the measuring milestone's own scope.</p>
            <ol class="plain">
                <li v-for="w in workItems()" :key="w"><span class="note">{{ w }}</span></li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="ceiling">
            <p class="eyebrow">05 — the ceiling</p>
            <h2>What the game itself publishes</h2>
            <p>The Benchmarks Game's own top entries are hand-tuned to the bone; this harness compares plainly written C, Rust and JavaScript instead. The game's numbers are recorded here as a ceiling, with the date they were read — recorded, not vendored. No source was taken from the site.</p>
            <p class="notice"><strong>These rows may not be divided into the ones above.</strong> The game runs every program at its own N — 50,000,000 steps of nbody where this dataset runs 25,000,000, fannkuch-redux at n = 12 against n = 11, binary-trees at depth 21 against 18 — on its own machine, with its own compilers. A ratio taken across the two would be arithmetic on unlike quantities. What the rows do answer is the one question they can: how far a hand-tuned entry sits from a plainly written one in the same language.</p>
            <div class="scroller">
            <table class="matrix">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Program</th><th>The game's N</th><th>Fastest published Rust</th><th>Fastest published C</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr v-for="row in ceilingRows()" :key="row[0]">
                        <th scope="row">{{ row[0] }}</th>
                        <td class="n">{{ row[1] }}</td>
                        <td>{{ row[2] }}</td>
                        <td>{{ row[3] }}</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            </div>
            <p class="cap">{{ ceilingFetched() }}. The N column comes from the game's per-language measurements page; the per-program pages carry the C comparison and do not print N, so the two were matched by the times agreeing. Recorded with that caveat rather than without it.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="reproduce">
            <p class="eyebrow">06 — checked</p>
            <h2>How to disagree with this page</h2>
            <p>Nothing here is a number this repository cannot produce again. The harness builds all five contestants, verifies each one against the game's expected output at the fixture size, times them, and writes the record this page is read from.</p>
            <div class="cmd"><code v-html="shellHtml(harnessCommand())"></code></div>
            <p class="note">It needs clang, rustc, node and wasmtime together and takes about fifteen minutes, which is why CI does not run it and the committed JSON is the record. What CI does gate on every push is that all eight programs still print the game's expected bytes: <code>cargo test -p vyrn-cli --test benchgame</code>.</p>
            <p class="cap">Machine and toolchain for every figure on this page: {{ benchMachine() }}.</p>
        </section>
    </main>
    <footer class="foot backstage" v-html="backstageFooter()"></footer>
</div>
</template>
